# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# RiftLedger environment
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Copy to .env.local for development. NEVER commit a filled-in copy — .env* is
# gitignored apart from this template.
#
# THE SITE RUNS WITH NONE OF THESE SET. The catalogue is bundled in the repo and
# prices fall back to the demo generator, so `npm run dev` works on a fresh clone
# with no configuration at all. Everything below unlocks something extra.

# ── Site ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Canonical origin, no trailing slash. Used for absolute URLs in metadata, the
# sitemap and OpenGraph tags. On Vercel this is inferred from the deployment URL
# if unset, but set it explicitly once a custom domain is attached — otherwise
# canonicals point at the *.vercel.app host.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL="https://riftledger.app"

# Shown on /about and in the footer for corrections and takedown requests.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CONTACT_EMAIL="hello@riftledger.app"

# ── Currency conversion (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Prices are STORED in USD cents; the header selector converts at render time.
# These override the built-in rates without a code change — handy when a rate
# has drifted far enough to look wrong. Same variable names as TCGEmpire, so a
# rate can be corrected on both sites at once. Defaults are in lib/currency.ts.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ because the conversion happens client-side, in <Money>. An
# invalid or non-positive value falls back to the built-in rate rather than
# rendering a nonsense price.
#
# Every converted figure is a REFERENCE price, not a quote — TCGplayer bills in
# USD regardless of what the selector says.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_USD_TO_EUR="0.92"
# NEXT_PUBLIC_USD_TO_GBP="0.79"
# NEXT_PUBLIC_USD_TO_AUD="1.5"
# NEXT_PUBLIC_USD_TO_CAD="1.37"
# NEXT_PUBLIC_USD_TO_SGD="1.35"
# NEXT_PUBLIC_USD_TO_NZD="1.65"

# ── Database (optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Needed for PERSISTED daily price snapshots (rather than the generated series)
# AND for accounts (see below — ACCOUNTS_ENABLED in lib/site.ts is just
# `!!DATABASE_URL`). Neon, Supabase or any Postgres works; the schema is in
# prisma/schema.prisma. Variable name matches TCGEmpire's so the two projects
# can share connection strings and tooling.
#
#   DATABASE_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST/DB?sslmode=require"
#
# Then: npm run db:push && npm run prices:import
# DATABASE_URL=""

# ── Accounts (optional, needs DATABASE_URL above) ────────────────────────────
# Email/password + Google/Discord sign-in. Until DATABASE_URL is set, /login and
# /signup render their forms disabled — same treatment as everything else below
# that needs a key. Once it is set, email/password sign-in works immediately;
# OAuth and outbound email are each independently optional on top of that.

# Secret used to sign session cookies. Generate a strong one for production:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# Outside production a dev-only fallback is used (with a console warning) if
# this is unset — production refuses to start without a real one.
# AUTH_SECRET="change-me-in-production"

# --- Sign in with Google (optional) ---
# Google Cloud Console → APIs & Services → Credentials → OAuth client (Web app).
# Authorised redirect URI: https://riftledger.app/api/auth/oauth/google/callback
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID="...apps.googleusercontent.com"
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET="..."

# --- Sign in with Discord (optional) ---
# Discord Developer Portal → New Application → OAuth2.
# Redirect: https://riftledger.app/api/auth/oauth/discord/callback   (scopes: identify, email)
# DISCORD_CLIENT_ID="..."
# DISCORD_CLIENT_SECRET="..."

# --- Transactional email for verification + password reset (optional) ---
# Without this, accounts still work but verification/reset emails just log a
# warning instead of sending — fine for local testing, not for real users.
# Also used for the daily price-drop digest (see PriceAlert below) — one key
# covers both, there is no separate one to set.
# resend.com → API Keys. Verify your sending domain (riftledger.app) for
# best deliverability; until then EMAIL_FROM can stay onboarding@resend.dev.
# RESEND_API_KEY="re_..."
# EMAIL_FROM="RiftLedger <noreply@riftledger.app>"

# --- Price-drop watch alerts (optional, needs DATABASE_URL + RESEND_API_KEY) ---
# "Watch" on a card page (src/components/CardActions.tsx) saves a PriceAlert row.
# /api/cron/price-alerts (see vercel.json's cron schedule) walks every watch
# daily, compares today's headline price to the last-seen one, and sends one
# digest email per subscriber for anything that dropped. With no DATABASE_URL
# the cron route no-ops cleanly (nothing to check) rather than erroring.

# ── TCGplayer (no config needed — nothing below this line to set) ───────────
# PRICES_ARE_DEMO flips to false automatically once src/data/prices.json has
# real entries in it — see lib/prices/live.ts's HAS_LIVE_PRICES and
# lib/prices/demo-flag.ts. There is no env var that controls this: the importer
# (src/lib/prices/tcgplayer.ts, run via `npm run prices:import`) reads
# TCGplayer's public search endpoint — the same one their own website uses —
# and needs no credentials. `npm run prices:import` writes that file locally;
# in production it's kept fresh by a GitHub Action that runs the same import
# and commits the snapshot daily (.github/workflows/refresh-prices.yml), which
# is what actually triggers the redeploy that removes the demo banners — not
# an env var in Vercel. See DEPLOYMENT.md step 6.
#
# TCGplayer's data is licensed, not public domain: attribution is required on
# every surface that displays it, it may not be presented as your own, and bulk
# redistribution is not permitted. Read their API terms
# (https://developer.tcgplayer.com/) before running the importer regularly.
# TCGPLAYER_PRIVATE_KEY=""

# ── TCGplayer affiliate / Impact ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# TCGplayer's affiliate program runs through Impact (impact.com). Outbound "Buy"
# links are wrapped through a deep-link base so purchases are credited.
#
# The code defaults to RiftCompare's APPROVED base, because both sites are on the
# same Impact account — so clicks credit correctly out of the box. Two things
# still need doing in the Impact dashboard before relying on it:
#   1. Add riftledger.app as a PROPERTY on the account. Sending traffic from
#      a domain the program has not been told about risks suspension even when
#      the account itself is correct.
#   2. Get this domain's verification token (below).
# Set this once TCGplayer issues a base specific to this site.
# TCGPLAYER_IMPACT_LINK="https://partner.tcgplayer.com/c/XXXXXXX/XXXXXXX/XXXXX"

# Impact site-ownership verification token, rendered as a <meta> in <head>.
# Per-domain — RiftCompare's token will NOT verify this one. Impact issues it
# when you add riftledger.app as a site. No default: without it the meta
# tag is simply omitted.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_IMPACT_SITE_VERIFICATION=""

# ── RiftCompare (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Sister site (see DATA_INTEGRATION.md) whose public, credential-free API backs
# two additions on the card page: the multi-store comparison grid and the real
# AU/NZ/UK/SG/CA "shopping outside the US" prices. No key needed — this only
# exists to point somewhere other than production RiftCompare (a staging
# mirror, or a local checkout) during development. Both features degrade to
# "not shown" (grid) or a plain search link (regional prices) if unset,
# unreachable, or the card has no match there — never a broken page.
# RIFTCOMPARE_API_URL="https://riftcompare.com/api/v1"

# ── Cardmarket (EU marketplace, optional) ────────────────────────────────────
# Stubbed vendor adapter — see src/lib/prices/providers/cardmarket.ts. As of
# writing Riftbound has no Cardmarket product line and their API needs an
# approved application (see the TODO(config) in that file), so setting this
# alone will NOT produce data; the adapter still returns "not configured"
# until the fetch itself is implemented. Present here so the config surface
# exists once both of those are true.
# CARDMARKET_APP_TOKEN=""

# ── Stripe (billing, optional) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Not wired up — see the TODO(config) in src/lib/plans.ts. Plan tiers and
# their limits are real and enforced; nothing currently moves a user between
# them automatically. Set this once a checkout + webhook route exist.
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=""

# ── Cron (optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Shared secret for scheduled HTTP endpoints under /api/cron/*, so they can't be
# triggered by anyone who guesses the path — currently just the daily price-drop
# alert digest above. (The separate daily TCGplayer price refresh is NOT this:
# it runs as a GitHub Action that commits a new snapshot straight to the repo —
# see .github/workflows/refresh-prices.yml — and needs no secret because it
# never exposes an HTTP endpoint.) Any long random string; Vercel Cron sends it
# automatically once set as a project env var and vercel.json's schedule is live.
# CRON_SECRET=""
